  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-8){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-8){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-4){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-4){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-4){e}^{t} \\\\  & =4t\\,{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t\\,{e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 4t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=0.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(0)=(4\\cdot 0-4){e}^{0}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=0,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4.$",
